{"date": "2026-11-02", "post_id": 1507038, "title": "A Long Cloud on Mars", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-2-a-long-cloud-on-mars/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> What\u2019s causing this unusually long cloud on <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/\">Mars</a>?\u00a0Known as the <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express/Mars_s_oddest_cloud_may_be_even_odder_than_we_thought\">Arsia Mons Elongated Cloud</a> (AMEC) and first recorded by NASA\u2019s <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-2/\">Viking Orbiter 2</a> in 1977, the <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/marss-most-mysterious-cloud-might-be-defying-textbook-cloud-physics\">strange cloud</a> has been imaged in greater detail more recently by <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>\u2019s robotic <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express\">Mars Express</a> spacecraft.\u00a0The <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/132160802@N06/49046308893/\">featured</a> <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2026/10/The_Arsia_Mons_Elongated_Cloud_as_seen_by_Mars_Express\">image</a> is from 2018. The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/how-do-clouds-form/\">cloud forms</a> only during <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-march-20-spring-equinox-at-teide-observatory/\">spring</a> and summer <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/seasons/\">seasons</a> and then only during morning hours. Made of water-ice, the most familiar process that causes <a href=\"https://airbornescience.nasa.gov/content/Sampling_the_composition_of_cirrus_ice_residuals\">cloud crystals</a> to <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/10387\">form around bits of dust</a> would fail due to the thinness and lack of dust high in the <a href=\"https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/mars-atmosphere-equation-english/\">Martian atmosphere</a>. <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41561-026-02089-9\">Leading speculation</a> holds that dust is not involved. After the cloud forms near the volcano <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsia_Mons\">Arsia Mons</a>, wind blows the cloud <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2012-april-22-flowing-barchan-sand-dunes-on-mars/\">downstream</a> \u2014 creating a tail that can extend over 1500 kilometers. Efforts to better understand <a href=\"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2020JE006517\">AMEC</a>s will continue, an understanding that may bolster a <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-tabby-cat-7GX5aICb5i4\">more complete view</a> of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/how-do-clouds-form/\">cloud formation</a> on <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\">Earth</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> eyeing a cat", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Enabling_Support/Space_Engineering_Technology/Deutsches_Zentrum_fuer_Luft-_und_Raumfahrt_DLR\">DLR</a>, <a href=\"https://www.geo.fu-berlin.de/en/geol/fachrichtungen/planet/projects/imaging/index.html\">FU Berlin</a>; <br>Processing &amp; <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Terms_and_conditions_of_use_of_images_and_videos_available_on_the_esa_website\">License</a>: <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/132160802@N06/\">J. Cowart</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Enabling_Support/Space_Engineering_Technology/Deutsches_Zentrum_fuer_Luft-_und_Raumfahrt_DLR\">DLR</a>, <a href=\"https://www.geo.fu-berlin.de/en/geol/fachrichtungen/planet/projects/imaging/index.html\">FU Berlin</a>; <br>Processing &amp; <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Terms_and_conditions_of_use_of_images_and_videos_available_on_the_esa_website\">License</a>: <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/132160802@N06/\">J. Cowart</a>", "alt": "Mars is seen from orbit with an orange landscape and two prominent volcanoes on the right. Extending  horizontally from the nearer volcano is a very long white cloud.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-2-a-long-cloud-on-mars/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MonsCloud_MarsExpress_1920.jpg?w=1920&h=1200&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 2 - A Long Cloud on Mars</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-2-a-long-cloud-on-mars/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: What\u2019s causing this unusually long cloud on Mars?\u00a0Known as the Arsia Mons Elongated Cloud (AMEC) and first recorded by NASA\u2019s Viking Orbiter 2 in 1977, the strange cloud has been imaged in greater detail more recently by ESA\u2019s robotic Mars Express spacecraft.\u00a0The featured image is from 2018. The cloud forms only during spring and summer seasons and then only during morning hours. Made of water-ice, the most familiar process that causes cloud crystals to form around bits of dust would fail due to the thinness and lack of dust high in the Martian atmosphere. Leading speculation holds that dust is not involved. After the cloud forms near the volcano Arsia Mons, wind blows the cloud downstream \u2014 creating a tail that can extend over 1500 kilometers. Efforts to better understand AMECs will continue, an understanding that may bolster a more complete view of cloud formation on Earth.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: eyeing a cat\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"a long cloud on mars\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"A Long Cloud on Mars\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-2-a-long-cloud-on-mars/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MonsCloud_MarsExpress_1920.jpg?w=1920&#038;h=1200&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MonsCloud_MarsExpress_1920.jpg?w=1920&#038;h=1200&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Mars is seen from orbit with an orange landscape and two prominent volcanoes on the right. Extending  horizontally from the nearer volcano is a very long white cloud.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-02T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-01T09:44:34-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"A Long Cloud on Mars\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MonsCloud_MarsExpress_1920.jpg?w=1920&#038;h=1200&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 2<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MonsCloud_MarsExpress_1920.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MonsCloud_MarsExpress_1920.jpg\" alt=\"Mars is seen from orbit with an orange landscape and two prominent volcanoes on the right. Extending  horizontally from the nearer volcano is a very long white cloud.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> A Long Cloud on Mars </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Enabling_Support/Space_Engineering_Technology/Deutsches_Zentrum_fuer_Luft-_und_Raumfahrt_DLR\">DLR</a>, <a href=\"https://www.geo.fu-berlin.de/en/geol/fachrichtungen/planet/projects/imaging/index.html\">FU Berlin</a>; <br>Processing &amp; <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Terms_and_conditions_of_use_of_images_and_videos_available_on_the_esa_website\">License</a>: <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/132160802@N06/\">J. Cowart</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> What\u2019s causing this unusually long cloud on <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/\">Mars</a>?\u00a0Known as the <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express/Mars_s_oddest_cloud_may_be_even_odder_than_we_thought\">Arsia Mons Elongated Cloud</a> (AMEC) and first recorded by NASA\u2019s <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-2/\">Viking Orbiter 2</a> in 1977, the <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/marss-most-mysterious-cloud-might-be-defying-textbook-cloud-physics\">strange cloud</a> has been imaged in greater detail more recently by <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>\u2019s robotic <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express\">Mars Express</a> spacecraft.\u00a0The <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/132160802@N06/49046308893/\">featured</a> <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2026/10/The_Arsia_Mons_Elongated_Cloud_as_seen_by_Mars_Express\">image</a> is from 2018. The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/how-do-clouds-form/\">cloud forms</a> only during <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-march-20-spring-equinox-at-teide-observatory/\">spring</a> and summer <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/seasons/\">seasons</a> and then only during morning hours. Made of water-ice, the most familiar process that causes <a href=\"https://airbornescience.nasa.gov/content/Sampling_the_composition_of_cirrus_ice_residuals\">cloud crystals</a> to <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/10387\">form around bits of dust</a> would fail due to the thinness and lack of dust high in the <a href=\"https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/mars-atmosphere-equation-english/\">Martian atmosphere</a>. <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41561-026-02089-9\">Leading speculation</a> holds that dust is not involved. After the cloud forms near the volcano <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsia_Mons\">Arsia Mons</a>, wind blows the cloud <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2012-april-22-flowing-barchan-sand-dunes-on-mars/\">downstream</a> \u2014 creating a tail that can extend over 1500 kilometers. Efforts to better understand <a href=\"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2020JE006517\">AMEC</a>s will continue, an understanding that may bolster a <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-tabby-cat-7GX5aICb5i4\">more complete view</a> of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/how-do-clouds-form/\">cloud formation</a> on <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\">Earth</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> eyeing a cat\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-1-colors-of-the-moon/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261102/html"}